explanation:
The asserted statement is the universally quantified arithmetic inequality L(n,r) = sum_{i=1}^{r}(5n/2 - 3i) - 3r + floor((n-3r)^2/4) <= floor(n^2/4) = R(n) over admissible (n,r). A single admissible pair with L > R refutes it, so it is finitely refutable.

I enumerated all 80,200 pairs with 1 <= n <= 400 and 1 <= r <= n using exact arithmetic (fractions.Fraction for the half-integer 5n/2 and the sum; integer floor division (n-3r)*(n-3r)//4 and n*n//4 for the floors; no floats in any comparison). 80,194 of the 80,200 pairs violate the inequality — only six degenerate tiny-n pairs satisfy it. Stratifying by every plausible reading of "admissible":
  - r >= 1, r <= n: 80,194 violations, smallest n=3, r=3 (LHS 9/2 > RHS 2);
  - r >= 1, 3r <= n: 26,598 violations, smallest n=5, r=1 (15/2 > 6);
  - r >= 1, n even: 40,197 violations, smallest n=4, r=2 (6 > 4);
  - r >= 1, 3r <= n and n even (strictest): 13,332 violations, smallest n=6, r=1 (11 > 9).
So under every reading the step fails, and it fails for essentially all admissible pairs rather than in some edge case. Structurally, the reason is that the sum contributes about 5nr/2, which is linear in n with slope 5r/2 and therefore dwarfs any savings from replacing floor(n^2/4) by floor((n-3r)^2/4) (that replacement saves only about (6nr - 9r^2)/4 = 3nr/2 - 9r^2/4, less than 5nr/2 minus lower-order terms). Concretely for r = 1 and n even the difference is exactly LHS - RHS = n - 4.

Consequently the "however, one can show that ..." inequality of line 0017 is false, the contradiction derived in lines 0016-0018 never arises, and line 0012's claim is not established by this argument. I did not examine any proposed witness, the original post, or any discussion; this search was conducted blind from the claim text alone.

